lesson g5-fractions-division-and-decimals v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=7 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Fractions, division and decimals"
  goal "Write a division as a fraction, and change a fraction into a decimal and a decimal into a fraction."

  look {
    scene 36x20 {
      decimalsquare square hundredths=75 write=true at=canvas(1, 1)
      longcake cake whole=16 from=0 to=16 candles=4 right-of=square gap=2
      library hall columns=6 below=cake gap=1
    }
    say """
      Three cakes are shared equally by four children at the library's feast. Each child cannot have
      a whole cake, but each can have a quarter of every cake: three quarters in all. So 3 ÷ 4 = 3/4,
      and any division of whole numbers can be written as a fraction, the top shared by the bottom.
      Going on with the division past the point turns the fraction into a decimal: 3 ÷ 4 = 0.75,
      seventy-five hundredths of the square. Read back, 0.75 is 75/100, which is 3/4 again.
      """
  }
  do {
    show quotient.rope a=3 b=5 easy-a=1 easy-b=3 hard-a=5 hard-b=8
    show quotient.pizzas a=7 b=4 easy-a=5 easy-b=3 hard-a=11 hard-b=6
    level easy {
      worked quotient.to-decimal n=3 d=4 level=medium
    }
    show quotient.to-decimal n=3 d=8 easy-n=1 easy-d=5 hard-n=7 hard-d=25
    show quotient.remainder a=29 b=4 easy-a=13 easy-b=2 hard-a=27 hard-b=8
    practice quotient.from-decimal count=1 seed=5
    show quotient.order t=1 easy-t=0 hard-t=2
    show quotient.ends t=5 easy-t=3 hard-t=4
    practice quotient.share-compare count=1 seed=7
    level hard {
      show quotient.ends t=6
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show quotient.unit-step d=8 k=5 easy-d=4 easy-k=3 hard-d=16 hard-k=7
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show quotient.count-ends n=10 easy-n=5 hard-n=20
  }
  remember {
    say "A fraction is a division: 3/4 is 3 ÷ 4. Divide the top by the bottom to get the decimal."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The fraction line is a division sign, and that one fact joins the two ways of writing a part.
    Sharing is the way in: three cakes among four children is three quarters each, not four thirds,
    so ask each time what is being shared and among how many. To make the decimal, the division
    carries on past the point with zeros; 3 ÷ 8 needs three places. A remainder is not the digit
    after the point: 17 ÷ 5 leaves 2 m, which is 2/5 of a metre each, 0.4, not 0.2. Only fractions
    whose bottom, in the simplest form, is made of twos and fives end as decimals, which is why 3/12
    ends and 4/14 does not. In the two-star question 5/8 is five lots of 1/8, so the board's 0.125
    is multiplied rather than divided again. In the three-star question a list by bottom, 2, 4, 5,
    8, 10, with only the tops that share nothing with the bottom, counts each fraction once. If the
    decimals are still guesswork, stay here another day with tenths and hundredths on the square.
    Off the page, share a bar of chocolate among the family and write each share three ways. This
    lesson meets Japan's grade 5 course of study, A(4)ア(イ), 「整数の除法の結果は，分数を用いると常に
    一つの数として表すことができることを理解すること。」
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level one or two metres of rope are shared, the loaves are shared among two or three children, 3/4 is worked once in the teacher's pen before 1/5, the chain is shared in halves, 13 m in two, the decimals to write are tenths and quarters, the list to order is the widest apart, the two-star uses 1 ÷ 4, and the three-star counts bottoms up to 5."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level four or five metres are shared among six to eight, the fractions to change are 7/25 and eighths, 27 m of chain is shared in eighths, the decimals simplify to twentieths and twenty-fifths, the list to order has 5/8 against 0.65, the question of stopping is asked twice with fractions that simplify, the two-star uses 1 ÷ 16, and the three-star counts bottoms up to 20."
  }
}
